every VLAN pair — found the network fully operational.

Router16's OSPF process is alive and healthy: it holds two Full adjacencies
(with mswitch0 at 10.2.2.2 via the bond0 LAG link, and with router7 at
172.30.1.2 via eth2), carries a complete LSDB of 19 LSAs, and has a fully
installed FIB that includes all Campus 1 subnets (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24)
and Campus 2 subnets (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24).
Router7, router17, router8, router10, and router11 all show full OSPF
convergence with correct route sets. Twelve independent ICMP ping tests —
covering every VLAN-to-VLAN combination in both directions — returned zero
packet loss. PC5 through PC14 (mswitch0 campus, VLANs 10/20/30) can all
reach 10.5.0.x and 10.6.0.x hosts, and vice versa.

The earlier apparent failure to reach "172.16.20.5" was simply a probe to an
unassigned IP address (PC9 is 172.16.20.9, PC10 is 172.16.20.10); pinging
those actual addresses succeeded immediately. The user's hypothesis that
"router16's OSPF died again" is demonstrably false.

Root cause: There is no fault. The helpdesk ticket #8044 is a false alarm —
the reporter may have confused a transient local issue or a stale memory of a
past event with the current network state. No corrective action on any device
is required.
